  ],
  "slug": "learning-omics-c285",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and publications apply masked autoencoders, negative binomial models, and foundation models to incomplete or sparse omics data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learned modeling applied to incomplete, sparse, or platform-dependent omics data. ReMAC extends ReMasker, a masked autoencoder, to classify spitzoid tumors from DNA methylation under complete and incomplete data. DeMixNB applies an embedded negative binomial distribution in semi-reference-based deconvolution of sparse-count miRNA-seq and spatial data. MISO predicts spatial transcriptomics from H&E histology across 72 Visium and 348 MOSAIC samples, while a benchmark of pathology foundation models addresses tertiary lymphoid structure detection in PDAC and HNSCC images. Compressed sensing (CISI) recovers 16 marker proteins from 8 composite channels in imaging mass cytometry, and sex-dependent expression enables pooled snRNA-seq deconvolution with 93-95% cell-sex accuracy. Across these items, the distinguishing feature is explicit handling of missingness, sparse counts, and cross-modality heterogeneity, implemented by distinct methods rather than one shared framework.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-28"
}
